> NetHack mostly just a game that old people play?

Not from what I can see, and certainly not if you spend a lot of time in
#nethack; there's no shortage of youthful players trying it out.  Though
they _do_ tend to scoff at the lack of graphics.

However, I would say that a lot of old/older people play it because,
well, we were there when it was one of the best there was.  Nethack is a
20-year old game, and in 1987 the graphical game market for PCs was an
entirely different front.  It was possible for a game to sacrifice
appearance for the sake of gameplay value, and Nethack did.

So what you're seeing is not a lot of old people _starting_ the game...
it's that a lot of us have just stayed with it.  I played a Rogue
variant for the first time around 1988 or so, and I was 16 then.  It'll
be interesting to see how many 16-year olds still play, say, 
CounterStrike in 20 years.

> What is the NetHack demographic, so to speak?

Well, realistically, geeks, nerds, and hackers.  There are certainly
exceptions to this rule, but the general case is largely what you'd
expect it to be.

